From 402d0cba0f42b777031a3bbcbdfc5d2052411121 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <claude@lmepisowifi.local>
Date: Wed, 9 Sep 2026 01:28:26 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] Fix ota.sh freeze on ISPs that black-hole GitHub
 release-asset CDN
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Some ISPs silently drop (rather than refuse) traffic to
release-assets.githubusercontent.com, the Fastly-anycast host GitHub
Release downloads redirect through. wget's own -T/-t don't reliably
bound that: it iterates every candidate IP with a full connect
timeout, across all retries, so a single fetch() call to a fully
black-holed host could hang ~5 minutes — matching the field report of
`./ota.sh cron` freezing solid. Confirmed against release-assets.
githubusercontent.com resolving fine but timing out on connect from
the affected connection, while github.com/raw.githubusercontent.com/
jsDelivr all worked.

Two changes, both additive/backward compatible:

1. fetch(): add a progress-based stall watchdog. Runs as a separate
   background process so the common (fast) case is a plain blocking
   `wait` with zero added latency — an earlier foreground-polling
   draft added a flat ~5s tax to every call, including instant
   successes and 404s, which would have slowed down every cron tick
   on every device; caught this via testing before landing it here.
   Kills wget only after 60s with no growth in the output file, so a
   slow-but-progressing transfer on a bad link still completes.
   Verified: fast success/failure now both return in ~0-1s (was
   forced to 5s in the draft), and a genuine stall against a local
   TCP black hole correctly kills and returns failure at ~60-65s.

2. do_apply(): prefer an optional `url_mirror` manifest field (a
   jsDelivr-served copy of the bundle) over the direct GitHub Release
   url, falling back to the release url on any failure — 404,
   unwarmed cache, oversized file, or no mirror published at all.
   jsDelivr is the same CDN path already relied on for manifest/
   favicon/module_ctl.sh and sits on different infrastructure than
   the Fastly range release-assets.githubusercontent.com uses, so it
   sidesteps this specific block. url_mirror is a brand new, optional
   manifest key — mval() simply returns empty for it on any manifest
   that doesn't publish one, so this is a no-op for that case. Reused
   the existing repo-pin allowlist (_url_ok(), factored out of the
   inline case-statement so both urls share one check) — the
   jsDelivr-url pattern it accepts was already there, anticipating
   exactly this move. Verified all four fetch outcomes (mirror ok,
   mirror fails -> primary ok, no mirror published -> primary as
   before, both fail -> reported failure) plus rejection of an
   out-of-repo url_mirror, against the actual code block.

release.yml: commit the base bundle into dist/ on main (same
tarball already built locally for the sha256 re-verify step, just
also git-added now) and publish its jsDelivr url as manifest.txt's
url_mirror. main, not the vVER tag, because manifest.txt/modules.txt
are already only ever committed to main after the tag is pushed —
matches existing precedent. Purge step extended to warm the new
path. hotspot/tailscale module downloads (modules.txt, module_ctl.sh)
are a separate path and untouched here — same fix could extend there
if module installs ever show the same freeze.

Co-Authored-By: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
---
 .github/workflows/release.yml | 13 ++++-
 ota.sh                        | 91 ++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++-----
 2 files changed, 90 insertions(+), 14 deletions(-)

diff --git a/.github/workflows/release.yml b/.github/workflows/release.yml
index 1f2ea13..e1f1506 100644
--- a/.github/workflows/release.yml
+++ b/.github/workflows/release.yml
@@ -85,6 +85,13 @@ jobs:
           ASSET_URL="https://github.com/$REPO/releases/download/v$VER/lmepisowifi-$VER.tar.gz"
           MOD_ASSET_URL="https://github.com/$REPO/releases/download/v$VER/lmepisowifi-hotspot-$VER.tar.gz"
           MOD_TS_ASSET_URL="https://github.com/$REPO/releases/download/v$VER/lmepisowifi-tailscale-$VER.tar.gz"
+          # jsDelivr mirror of the base bundle: committed into dist/ on main
+          # below, so this resolves once this job's commit lands (same @main
+          # pattern already used for manifest.txt/modules.txt/CHANGELOG.md).
+          # Sidesteps ISPs that black-hole the Fastly range GitHub Release
+          # downloads redirect through; ota.sh falls back to ASSET_URL if
+          # this ever 404s or fails.
+          MIRROR_URL="https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/gh/$REPO@main/$TAR"
 
           # --- Step 1: publish the release + tarball FIRST ---
           # manifest.txt is not committed yet, so no device can see an sha256
@@ -130,6 +137,7 @@ jobs:
           {
             echo "version=$VER"
             echo "url=$ASSET_URL"
+            echo "url_mirror=$MIRROR_URL"
             echo "sha256=$SHA"
             echo "notes=Release $VER"
             echo "nodemcu_version=$FW"
@@ -153,16 +161,19 @@ jobs:
           } > modules.txt
           git config user.name  github-actions
           git config user.email github-actions@github.com
-          git add manifest.txt modules.txt hotspot/firmware/coin_nodemcu.bin
+          git add manifest.txt modules.txt hotspot/firmware/coin_nodemcu.bin "$TAR"
           git commit -m "release $VER (nodemcu fw $FW)" || echo "nothing to commit"
           git push origin HEAD:main
           
       - name: Purge jsDelivr cache
         run: |
           REPO="${GITHUB_REPOSITORY}"
+          VER="${GITHUB_REF_NAME#v}"
           echo "Purging CHANGELOG.md..."
           curl -sL "https://purge.jsdelivr.net/gh/${REPO}@main/CHANGELOG.md"
           echo -e "\nPurging manifest.txt..."
           curl -sL "https://purge.jsdelivr.net/gh/${REPO}@main/manifest.txt"
           echo -e "\nPurging modules.txt..."
           curl -sL "https://purge.jsdelivr.net/gh/${REPO}@main/modules.txt"
+          echo -e "\nPurging dist/ bundle mirror..."
+          curl -sL "https://purge.jsdelivr.net/gh/${REPO}@main/dist/lmepisowifi-${VER}.tar.gz"
diff --git a/ota.sh b/ota.sh
index 48a04b3..9243d3a 100755
--- a/ota.sh
+++ b/ota.sh
@@ -161,6 +161,14 @@ cdnify() { # cdnify <url> -> prints CDN url (or the original url unchanged)
 }
 
 # wget wrapper: HTTPS-only, retries, timeouts, writable -O target, cert handling.
+# Backgrounded + progress-polled: some ISPs black-hole (rather than refuse)
+# traffic to specific GitHub-adjacent hosts — e.g. release-assets.
+# githubusercontent.com, the Fastly-anycast host GitHub Release downloads
+# redirect through — and DNS/TCP hangs there aren't always bound cleanly by
+# -T/-t alone. This kills only on a genuine stall (output file not growing
+# for 60s), so a slow-but-working transfer on a bad link still completes.
+# sleep/kill/wait are already used elsewhere in this script, so this adds
+# no new tool dependency.
 fetch() { # fetch <url> <outfile>
     _u=$(cdnify "$1")
     _wf="--https-only -t 3 -T 30 --retry-connrefused -U lmepisowifi-ota"
@@ -169,7 +177,40 @@ fetch() { # fetch <url> <outfile>
     else
         _wf="$_wf --no-check-certificate"
     fi
-    wget $_wf -q -O "$2" "$_u"
+    mkdir -p "$(dirname "$2")" 2>/dev/null
+    : > "$2" 2>/dev/null
+    wget $_wf -q -O "$2" "$_u" &
+    _fw_pid=$!
+    # Stall watch runs as its own background process so the common case
+    # (wget finishes on its own) is a plain blocking `wait` below with no
+    # added latency — this used to poll in the foreground with a flat 5s
+    # floor on every call, which is not what we want. This process just
+    # kills wget after 60s of no growth in the output file; it never
+    # touches fetch()'s own return value.
+    ( _fw_stall=0; _fw_last=-1
+      while kill -0 "$_fw_pid" 2>/dev/null; do
+          sleep 5
+          kill -0 "$_fw_pid" 2>/dev/null || exit 0
+          _fw_now=$(wc -c < "$2" 2>/dev/null); _fw_now=${_fw_now:-0}
+          if [ "$_fw_now" = "$_fw_last" ]; then
+              _fw_stall=$((_fw_stall + 5))
+          else
+              _fw_stall=0
+          fi
+          _fw_last="$_fw_now"
+          if [ "$_fw_stall" -ge 60 ]; then
+              kill -9 "$_fw_pid" 2>/dev/null
+              log "fetch: killed stalled wget (no progress 60s) — $_u"
+              exit 0
+          fi
+      done
+    ) &
+    _fw_watch=$!
+    wait "$_fw_pid"
+    _fw_rc=$?
+    kill "$_fw_watch" 2>/dev/null
+    wait "$_fw_watch" 2>/dev/null
+    return $_fw_rc
 }
 
 # Ensure hotspot/img/favicon.ico exists, fetching the repo's default from
@@ -282,6 +323,19 @@ ensure_tailscale_www2() {
 # parse a key=value line from the manifest (strips CR)
 mval() { sed -n "s/^$1=//p" "$DL/manifest.txt" | tr -d '\r' | head -1; }
 
+# SECURITY: pin a download url to our own repo. Accepts either a GitHub
+# Release asset or a jsDelivr-served file from the same repo tree
+# (cdn.jsdelivr.net/gh/OWNER/REPO@...). Shared by do_apply() for both the
+# primary url and the optional url_mirror, so the two allowlists can't
+# drift apart.
+_url_ok() {
+    case "$1" in
+        "https://github.com/$OTA_REPO/releases/download/"*) return 0 ;;
+        "https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/gh/$OTA_REPO@"*) return 0 ;;
+        *) return 1 ;;
+    esac
+}
+
 # strictly-newer compare using dotted numeric fields: ver_gt A B  -> true if A>B
 ver_gt() {
     _a="$1"; _b="$2"
@@ -370,26 +424,37 @@ do_apply() {
         set_status "failed"; log "ERROR: cannot fetch manifest"; return 1
     fi
     _lat=$(mval version); _url=$(mval url); _sum=$(mval sha256); _notes=$(mval notes)
+    _murl=$(mval url_mirror)
     [ -n "$_want" ] && [ "$_want" != "$_lat" ] && {
         log "NOTE: requested $_want but manifest latest is $_lat — installing manifest version"
     }
     if [ -z "$_lat" ] || [ -z "$_url" ] || [ -z "$_sum" ]; then
         set_status "failed"; log "ERROR: manifest incomplete (need version/url/sha256)"; return 1
     fi
-
-    # SECURITY: pin the download to our own repo. Accept both a GitHub Release
-    # asset and a jsDelivr-served file from the same repo tree
-    # (cdn.jsdelivr.net/gh/OWNER/REPO@...), so tarballs can be moved onto the CDN
-    # later without touching this guard. The manifest sha256 is verified below
-    # regardless of source, so a tampered download is always rejected.
-    case "$_url" in
-        "https://github.com/$OTA_REPO/releases/download/"*) : ;;
-        "https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/gh/$OTA_REPO@"*) : ;;
-        *) set_status "failed"; log "ERROR: refusing url outside repo (Releases or jsDelivr): $_url"; return 1 ;;
-    esac
+    if ! _url_ok "$_url"; then
+        set_status "failed"; log "ERROR: refusing url outside repo (Releases or jsDelivr): $_url"; return 1
+    fi
 
     set_status "downloading"; log "downloading $_lat"
-    if ! fetch "$_url" "$DL/bundle.tar.gz"; then
+    # Prefer the jsDelivr mirror when the manifest publishes one (url_mirror
+    # is optional — older manifests simply don't have it, and mval() returns
+    # empty, so this block is skipped entirely and behaviour is unchanged).
+    # It's the same CDN path already relied on for manifest/favicon/
+    # module_ctl.sh, and it sidesteps ISPs that black-hole the
+    # release-assets.githubusercontent.com (Fastly) range that plain GitHub
+    # Release downloads redirect through. Any failure — 404, unwarmed cache,
+    # oversized file — falls straight back to the direct Release url below,
+    # so nothing regresses when the mirror isn't available or isn't helping.
+    _dl_ok=1
+    if [ -n "$_murl" ] && _url_ok "$_murl"; then
+        log "downloading via jsDelivr mirror"
+        if fetch "$_murl" "$DL/bundle.tar.gz"; then
+            _dl_ok=0
+        else
+            log "mirror download failed — falling back to $_url"
+        fi
+    fi
+    if [ "$_dl_ok" -ne 0 ] && ! fetch "$_url" "$DL/bundle.tar.gz"; then
         set_status "failed"; log "ERROR: download failed"; notify "OTA: download of $_lat failed"; return 1
     fi
 
-- 
